  "question": {
    "text": "<p>In an experiment to measure the focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo></math> of a convex lens, the magnitude of object distance<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></math> and the image distance<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></math> are measured with reference to the focal point of the lens. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>x</mi></math> plot is shown in figure. The focal length of the lens is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>cm</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/a936d3b9-beb6-4c11-9389-5a775ddcf8f7.png\" style=\"width: 350px; height: 296px;\"/>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "20",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/a936d3b9-beb6-4c11-9389-5a775ddcf8f7.png\" style=\"width: 350px; height: 296px;\"/></p><p>From Newton's formula for lens, we can write</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p>Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p></blockquote>",
    "image": null
  },
